{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01NxAZW2wi5zx7yRMNeWmTvE","type":"tool_result","content":"40\t// The C implementation writes through `printf` from `<stdio.h>`.  We call the\n41\t// very same function so that the emitted bytes -- and the stdio buffering /\n42\t// flush-at-exit behaviour that decides *when* those bytes appear -- are\n43\t// bit-for-bit identical to the original library.\n44\tunsafe extern \"C\" {\n45\t    fn printf(format: *const c_char, ...) -> c_int;\n46\t}\n47\t\n48\t/// C: `printf(\"%02x\\n\", charHex);`\n49\t///\n50\t/// Note the (deliberately preserved) quirk of the original: `charHex` has type\n51\t/// `char`, so it undergoes the default argument promotion to `int` before being\n52\t/// consumed by `%x`, which reinterprets it as `unsigned int`.  On targets where\n53\t/// `char` is signed (e.g. x86-64 Linux) a negative value therefore prints as\n54\t/// eight hex digits -- `driver(0x7f)` yields `ffffff80`, not `80`.  This is not\n55\t/// a bug we fix here; it is reproduced exactly.\n56\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n57\tpub extern \"C\" fn printHexCharLine(charHex: c_char) {\n58\t    // b\"%02x\\n\\0\" -- identical format string to the C source.\n59\t    const FORMAT: &[u8; 6] = b\"%02x\\n\\0\";\n60\t    unsafe {\n61\t        printf(FORMAT.as_ptr() as *const c_char, charHex as c_int);\n62\t    }\n63\t}\n64\t\n65\t/// C:\n66\t/// ```c\n67\t/// void driver(char data)\n68\t/// {\n69\t///     char result = data + 1;\n70\t///     printHexCharLine(result);\n71\t/// }\n72\t/// ```\n73\t///\n74\t/// `data + 1` is evaluated in `int` and then converted back to `char`; GCC/Clang\n75\t/// implement that narrowing conversion as a two's-complement truncation, which\n76\t/// `wrapping_add` reproduces (so `driver(0x7f)` produces `result == -128`).\n77\t#[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n78\tpub extern \"C\" fn driver(data: c_char) {\n79\t    let result: c_char = data.wrapping_add(1);\n80\t    printHexCharLine(result);\n81\t}\n82\t"}]},"