{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_mwicx4aqz7etbk3mmrwdmrlzzyhbtwkh4e6itt6bnllentfrcwhq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass, and the process found **two real bugs**.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is tiny — two exported functions, `void driver(char)` and `void printHexCharLine(char)`. `driver` computes `data + 1` and forwards to the printer, which does `printf(\"%02x\\n\", charHex)`. There is no state, no options, no pointers, and (verified by grep) **zero** conditionals, error returns, or asserts.\n\n## Phase A artifacts\n\n- `SYMBOLS.md` — `nm -D` parity table; symbol diff empty from the start.\n- `ERRORS.md` — 7 rows. The C has an *empty explicit* error surface, so each row documents the specific ABI-level non-rejection behavior instead of hand-waving \"both fail\".\n- `CONFIGS.md` — 23 rows over the axes the C actually distinguishes (`%02x` padding classes, sign boundary, wrap-to-zero, exhaustive 256-value sweep, batching/ordering, full-width register ABI, composed pipeline).\n\n## Two real divergences found and fixed\n\n**1. Argument-register truncation (release-only).** gcc's callee doesn't trust the upper 24 bits of `%edi` — it re-truncates (`mov %al`) then sign-extends. The Rust declared `extern \"C\" fn(c_char)`, which attaches LLVM's `signext` and *assumes* the caller sign-extended; `--release` then dropped the truncation (`mov %edi,%esi`). A caller passing `255` through an `fn(int)` prototype got `ff` from Rust but `ffffffff` from C. This is exactly the out-of-range-value-across-FFI class the brief flagged. Fixed by taking `c_int` and truncating explicitly.\n\n**2. Lost PLT interposability.** The C's `driver` calls `printHexCharLine` via the PLT, so an `LD_PRELOAD`ed definition replaces it. LLVM inlined the Rust callee in release, silently destroying that. Fixed by routing the call through a symbol relocation (`R_X86_64_64` against `printHexCharLine`) read with `read_volatile`.\n\nBoth bugs were **invisible in debug builds** — so `run_all.sh` now runs every feature combination against both profiles (216 green test executions = 36 tests × 3 combos × 2 profiles).\n\n## Two process notes worth flagging\n\n- **Everything passed on my first run.** Since passing tests prove nothing on their own, I added `mutation_check.sh`: 10 injected bugs, each of which the suite must catch, plus 1 *provably equivalent* mutant it must **not** flag (verified by disassembly that codegen is instruction-identical). My first mutation run reported three false \"all clear\" results — that was a flaw in my script (debug-only), and fixing it is what exposed bug #1's blind spot.\n- **I briefly corrupted `src/lib.rs`.** I stashed a backup in `$TMPDIR`, which changes between tool calls, so the restore failed and sed mutations accumulated. There is no git here. I rebuilt the file from the version in my context and verified it. The scripts now keep backups inside the repo directory. Nothing in `c_src/` was touched.\n\nCaveat on scope: `libloading` couldn't be fetched (network blocked), so all cargo commands use `--offline` against the crate already in the local cache. Both findings are x86-64 System V specific; the same C on a platform with unsigned `char` would need different expected values."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":288,"cache_read_input_tokens":151754,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":288,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"83e365a9-f7ed-4ae2-b58c-3909f34c7cf4","uuid":"95dbf0f4-3cef-41a1-816b-58dbb79a7cda","timestamp":"2026-08-28T12:10:08.760Z"}
